--TEST--
`Core\Html::sanitize` rebuilds a document from a closed allowlist, so an element, an attribute or a URL scheme it does not hold leaves no tag and no escaped text either — `rule:core-classes/html-sanitize`, `rule:security/launderers-are-sink-named`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The second launderer on this class, and the one for markup: `escape` takes
// text that must arrive as text, this takes markup that must arrive as markup.
// Both name the same sink, which is what `rule:security/launderers-are-sink-named` asks of a
// launderer — narrow, and named for the one place its answer is safe.
tainted string $submitted =
    "<p>Hello <b>there</b>.</p>" .
    "<script>steal(document.cookie)</script>" .
    "<p onclick=\"boom()\">Bye <a href=\"javascript:alert(1)\">now</a>" .
    " and <a href=\"/docs\">here</a>.</p>";

Core\Html\Markup $clean = Core\Html::sanitize($submitted);
string $source = Core\Html::toSource($clean, "asking what the rebuild kept");
echo $source, "\n";

// Four different reasons for something to be missing from that line, and each
// is the allowlist rather than a judgement about the input. `<script>` went
// with its content, because its character data was never text and putting it
// back into a document would be writing a program into prose. `onclick` was
// never written, because the list grants no handler. The `javascript:` address
// was not written while the relative one was, because the test is on the
// scheme and the schemes that fetch are the whole of it.
echo Core\Str::contains($source, "steal") ? "y" : "n",
     Core\Str::contains($source, "onclick") ? "y" : "n",
     Core\Str::contains($source, "javascript") ? "y" : "n",
     Core\Str::contains($source, "/docs") ? "y" : "n",
     "\n";

// And the half that tells a rebuild from a filter. A filter that escapes what
// it does not like leaves the source in the page as visible text; a rebuild
// has nowhere to put it, so an element with no place in the grammar leaves no
// `&lt;` behind either.
echo Core\Str::contains($source, "&lt;") ? "y" : "n",
     Core\Str::contains($source, "&gt;") ? "y" : "n",
     "\n";
--EXPECT--
<p>Hello <b>there</b>.</p><p>Bye <a>now</a> and <a href="/docs">here</a>.</p>
nnny
nn
